# fixedbugs, defect 068, panels 167 to 171, closed 2026-09-21.
#
# SYMPTOM: a wrong answer at exit 0 with ZERO AddressSanitizer reports, on any
# platform. C holds a field's address, the program rewrites the record two
# lines below, and C reads bytes the program never meant it to: 72, then 1. No
# memory rule is broken — the storage is alive and its contents changed — so no
# sanitizer can ever see it; only the program's meaning is wrong.
#
# CAUSE: defect 066's. Nothing related C's retention to the bytes' life, and
# the lend into `k_register` was admitted although C keeps the address. Panel
# 168's completeness critic found the corrupting action is a HEROES assignment,
# in the same function, to the very binding whose field was lent — caller-side,
# where static enforcement exists everywhere — which is why the two defects
# were one class after all.
#
# FIX: panel 171's default. `k_register` does not say `lent`, so the lend is
# refused where it is made, before the assignment that would have corrupted
# it. A binding whose field was lent to a `lent` parameter may be rewritten
# freely afterwards, because C kept nothing: `k_sum` below is quiet.

extern "keeper.h"
    record KSlot
        name: u8[8]
        id: i64
    function k_register(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64)
    function k_read_later() -> i64
    function k_sum(p: ptr counted_by n lent, n: i64) -> i64

function main()
    s: KSlot @ KSlot(name: [72, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0], id: 0)
    k_register(p: s.name.ptr(), n: 8)  #~ lend_kept
    print(k_read_later())
    s @ KSlot(name: [1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0], id: 1)
    print(k_read_later())
    print(k_sum(p: s.name.ptr(), n: 8))
